From GDPR point of view this is at least a grey zone. The information which apps are and are not installed on a users' phone is considered to be personal information, especially if you have other data on that person (such as phone number, name etc.). For example, would I want anyone to know that I have Grindr installed on my phone?

Therefore we need to know on what basis we are using this data. Do we have consent? I suppose not and also that we don't intend to ask for it. Do we use legitimate interest? This information is useful for us and enables us to provide better services. Can we document how this info helps us, and how it is not too intrusive to the users?

Do we know if our friends and comrade use this feature? 

@jevgeni please provide or get a team member to provide why we use this data and how it is useful to us.
@anna: do we apply google's best practices
@here: who knows if our friends use this as well?

In short: if this is really helpful to us, if our friends do it as well, and if we respect Google's [Privacy Best Practices|https://developer.android.com/privacy/best-practices], it may be worth taking the risk.

